                <h2>Three Common Dilemmas in Budget Allocation for Independent Sites</h2>
<p>In serving cross-border teams, we've found many independent site sellers fall into two extremes in budget allocation: either 'all in' on a single channel, or 'sprinkle a little everywhere'. Both approaches cause problems.</p>
<p>The following checklist helps you establish a clear budget decision framework, with actionable check items at each node.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Budget Decision Checklist: 5 Nodes from Launch to Stability</h2>
<h3>Node 1: Pre-Launch — Setting the Total Budget Framework</h3>
<p>Before any ad spending, answer these 3 questions:</p>
<ul>
<li>[ ] What is your monthly GMV target? (Used to back-calculate required traffic)</li>
<li>[ ] What is your target ROAS? (Used to calculate required ad spend)</li>
<li>[ ] How long can you sustain maximum monthly losses? (Determines your test budget ceiling)</li>
</ul>
<blockquote>
<p><strong>Reference Formula</strong>: Monthly Ad Budget = Target GMV ÷ Target ROAS<br />
Example: Target monthly GMV of 500K, ROAS target of 3x, so monthly budget is approximately 166K.</p>
</blockquote>
<hr />
<h3>Node 2: Cold Start Period (0-30 Days) — Initial Channel Selection and Testing</h3>
<p>The core task at this stage is to 'test quickly and find what converts'.</p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Channel</th>
<th>Recommended Ratio</th>
<th>Notes</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Meta (Facebook/Instagram)</td>
<td>40-50%</td>
<td>Large traffic, good for product testing</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Google Ads (Search + Shopping)</td>
<td>20-30%</td>
<td>Clear intent, high conversion rate</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>TikTok</td>
<td>10-20%</td>
<td>Creative-driven, suitable for new brands</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Testing Reserve</td>
<td>10-15%</td>
<td>For discovering new channel opportunities</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p><strong>Cold Start Checklist</strong>:</p>
<ul>
<li>[ ] Set up at least 2 ad campaigns and 3-5 ad groups per channel</li>
<li>[ ] Focus on 'learning phase' data for the first 3 days, avoid premature adjustments</li>
<li>[ ] Review at day 7, pause ad groups with high spend but no conversions</li>
<li>[ ] Confirm primary channel at day 14, increase investment</li>
</ul>
<hr />
<h3>Node 3: Optimization Period (30-90 Days) — Dynamic Adjustment and Channel Focus</h3>
<p>After the testing phase, shift budget toward channels that 'data proves effective'.</p>
<p><strong>Adjustment Decision Checklist</strong>:</p>
<ul>
<li>[ ] Calculate CPA and ROAS for each channel, eliminate below-average channels</li>
<li>[ ] Transfer 70% of 'inefficient channel' budget to 'efficient channels'</li>
<li>[ ] Reserve 5-10% budget for continuous testing of new ad formats (e.g., Retargeting, Dynamic Creative)</li>
<li>[ ] Update channel budget allocation table weekly</li>
</ul>
<blockquote>
<p>Experience from a utility app team: After the testing phase, they found Facebook shopping ads had significantly higher ROAS than search ads, so they reduced search budget from 30% to 15%, improving overall ROAS by 0.5x within two months.</p>
</blockquote>
<hr />
<h3>Node 4: Stable Period (90+ Days) — Scaling and Risk Management</h3>
<p><strong>Reference Budget Allocation Ratios</strong> (Mature Stage):</p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Type</th>
<th>Ratio</th>
<th>Purpose</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Primary Acquisition Channels</td>
<td>50-60%</td>
<td>Ensure GMV baseline</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Retargeting Channels</td>
<td>15-20%</td>
<td>Improve conversion rate, reduce cart abandonment</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>New Channel Testing</td>
<td>10-15%</td>
<td>Find second growth curve</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Brand Advertising</td>
<td>5-10%</td>
<td>Long-term asset accumulation</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p><strong>Risk Control Checklist</strong>:</p>
<ul>
<li>[ ] No single channel exceeds 40% of total budget (prevent platform policy risks)</li>
<li>[ ] Set daily budget caps to prevent overspending</li>
<li>[ ] Maintain account balance of at least 3 days' spend buffer</li>
<li>[ ] Start incremental testing 1 month before peak season to build creative reserves for high-traffic periods</li>
</ul>
<hr />
<h3>Node 5: Daily Execution — Budget Monitoring and Adjustment</h3>
<p><strong>Daily Check (5 minutes)</strong>:</p>
<ul>
<li>[ ] Is spending normal across all channels? Any abnormal spikes?</li>
<li>[ ] Are primary campaigns in learning phase? Any 'account受限' warnings?</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Weekly Review</strong>:</p>
<ul>
<li>[ ] Spend vs. output (ROAS/CPA) for each channel</li>
<li>[ ] Which ad groups are entering 'performance decline'?</li>
<li>[ ] Creative fatigue: CTR drop exceeding 20% requires new creatives</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Monthly Assessment</strong>:</p>
<ul>
<li>[ ] Is budget allocation aligned with goals? Deviations exceeding 15% need recalibration</li>
<li>[ ] Is there a corresponding budget adjustment plan for peak/off-peak seasons?</li>
</ul>
<hr />
<h2>FAQ</h2>
<p><strong>Q: Just started my independent site with a small budget. How should I allocate it?</strong></p>
<p>Focus testing on one channel until that channel's ROAS stabilizes above 2.5x, then expand. Don't spread thin when budget is limited, otherwise every channel won't get enough data volume.</p>
<p><strong>Q: How should budget be adjusted during peak season?</strong></p>
<p>Start incremental testing 30-45 days in advance, increase budget to 1.5-2x normal levels 2 weeks before peak season, and raise bids to capture more impressions.</p>
<p><strong>Q: Any recommendations for account balance management and top-ups?</strong></p>
<p>Establish a 'balance warning' mechanism and top up when account balance falls below 5 days' spend. Avoid concentrated top-ups at month-end that could cause account suspension and affect ad delivery.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Summary</h2>
<p>Budget allocation isn't a one-time decision—it's a continuous optimization process. Core principles: let data speak, don't rely on intuition; leave room for testing, don't pursue immediate perfection; review regularly, don't set and forget.</p>
